  "answer": "I rewrote the two writable files.\n\n**`registry.mjs` — `resolveFromRegistry(path, records)`**\n- Validation: throws `TypeError` unless `path` is a string, nonempty, and starts with `/` (previously the empty-string check was ordered after `startsWith`, and the message/behavior is now explicit).\n- Matching policy now follows the spec instead of declaration order:\n  - Scans all records; the **first matching static** record returns immediately (static always beats dynamic, regardless of order).\n  - The **first matching dynamic** record is remembered (not returned early), so a later static can still win.\n  - The **first fallback** is remembered and only used if neither static nor dynamic matched.\n  - Returns `null` when nothing matches and there is no fallback.\n- No mutation of `records`; each call builds fresh `params` objects (`{}` for static/fallback, a copy of the dynamic params), so results are independent across calls.\n- Path text is compared exactly (case sensitive, trailing slash significant) via the existing `matchStatic`/`matchDynamic` helpers, which already treat `:name` as a whole nonempty segment and preserve original segment values.\n\n**`entry.mjs` — `dispatch(path)`**\n- Now delegates solely to `resolveFromRegistry(path, defaultRoutes)`, so the production route table is the only source of truth and invalid paths throw `TypeError` from the resolver. The `legacyResolve` shortcut was removed from the entry point.\n\n**Unchanged:** `legacy.mjs` (still returns `{ route: 'legacy-help', params: {} }` only for `/old/help`), the adapters, utils, route helpers, and fixtures.\n\n**Checked:** I read all relevant files and reasoned through the fixture cases — `/users/new` → `user-new` (static beats the earlier dynamic), `/users/Ada` → `user-detail` with `{ id: 'Ada' }`, `/health` → `health`, `/missing` → `not-found` fallback, and `resolveFromRegistry('/x', [])` → `null`. No tests were executed (no shell/test runner available), so I cannot claim the suite passes.",
